      "content": "Yes, that is a valid critique and one that critics of \"surge of sovereignty\" campaigners strongly argue: the \"national security\" and \"anti-extremism\" justification often becomes a convenient mask for political censorship. When the line between legitimate safety measures and suppressing dissent blurs, it severely undermines the very freedoms the internet is meant to protect. The real risk lies in the selective enforcement where oppressive rhetoric is allowed while disagreement is criminalized, making the security argument effectively a tool for regime control.",
